WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.840
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.139
Today we’ll be learning about the molecular
interactions that stabilize secondary structure.

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:12.600
We’ll use the structure from last time.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.930
These proteins interact to modulate the movement
of bacterial flagella.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.000
And as an added bonus, they contain all the
basic secondary structure elements I want

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.869
to teach you about!

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:31.160
Load the structure as usual, 1A0o, and display
the sequence.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.989
Now, color by secondary structure.

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:38.570
We’re going to keep coming back to this
view to explore the different types of secondary

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.730
structure in this model, so let’s save a
scene.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.629
Scene dropdown menu, store, F1.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:48.879
Right click on F1 and rename it.

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:50.790
I’ll call it overall.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.550
We’ll explore an alpha helix first.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.680
Let’s rotate and try to find the longest
one.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.970
Maybe this one?

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.180
Click on a residue in the helix.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:08.500
Notice how we see a highlight on the sequence
display, but the scroll bar is outside of

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.500
it.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.090
Scroll until we can see the residue we’ve
selected.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:19.180
Great, now let’s expand our selection by
clicking and dragging over the other residues

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.770
that make up this red colored helix.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.000
Now, one of my favorite command combos.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.420
Sele, H, unselected.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.290
Then, rename the selection to helix to save
it.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:39.600
Helices have distinct hydrogen bonding patterns
involving their backbone atoms, which PyMOL

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.119
calls the main chain.

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.530
We’ll need to see the oxygen and nitrogen
atoms to show this.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.040
Let’s color the helix with CPK coloring
and show sticks.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:56.771
I’m going to make sure my N terminus is
on top by clicking on the leftmost amino acid

00:01:56.771 --> 00:01:59.310
in the sequence.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.860
And center it.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:06.619
All the backbone carbonyls—C double bond
O groups—now point downward.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:11.440
They’ll hydrogen bond to a nitrogen atom
of another amino acid.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.950
If it’s an alpha helix, the hydrogen bonding
will follow an i+4 pattern.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:24.220
The means the residue closer to the N-terminus
hydrogen bonds to the residue that is four

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.420
positions away.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:32.450
For example, if it’s residue 100, it will
H bond to residue 104.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.379
Let’s add these backbone–backbone interactions.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:41.650
Find, polar contacts, just intra main chain.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:46.880
Ok, that’s more than expected, but it’s
hard to see anything at all.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.250
Let’s start by hiding the cartoon.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.030
Still pretty busy and overwhelming.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:57.670
Since it’s only the main chain interactions
we’re looking for, let’s also hide the

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.480
amino acid side chains.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.780
That’s better.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:08.420
Now, let’s analyze a residue making only
one contact to determine the type of helix.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:14.250
Click on the two interacting atoms, and the
residues will highlight in the sequence.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:26.910
It’s K122 interacting with K126. 122 plus
4 is 126 so this is indeed an alpha helix.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:32.310
We’re also just seeing some secondary close
contacts with all these lines.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:37.840
Let’s produce an image that just shows only
the main stabilizing interaction for an alpha

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:38.840
helix.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.580
Well, we don’t want all these secondary
measurements.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.770
We can use the measurement wizard to pick
only the interactions we want to show.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.690
In the dropdown menu: Wizard, measurement.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:04:01.030
We can now trace over the vertically oriented
i+4 interactions.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:07.610
When we deselect the main chain polar contacts,
we see the i+4 interactions we’ve measured.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:12.890
If we measure the whole helix, there are going
to be a lot of measurements that fill up the

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.510
whole names panel.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.359
We’ve got a couple options to neaten this
up.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:20.699
You can group objects in the names panel.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:27.970
So let’s take measure 1, A, group, new group.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:32.510
Now let’s add measurement 2 and 3 to the
group.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:36.600
Now, we can operate on the grouped objects
together.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.600
Let’s recolor them.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:43.070
There’s another way to achieve this that
I really like, but if you mis click you’ll

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.160
have to start over.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.509
First let’s delete our grouped object.

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:53.009
Now, the purple parts of the measurement wizard
have built in menus.

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:58.229
Click on “create new object”, and change
this to “merge with previous”.

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:02.289
Now, I’ll start where we did before for
the first measurement.

00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:06.720
Then, carefully rotate to capture the others.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.979
Notice there’s just one measurement in the
names panel, and we’re adding to it.

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:15.229
Let’s rotate and try to measure them all.

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:21.260
Ok, I think we’ve got them!

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.530
Click done.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:29.919
And now we have one clean object that I’ll
rename to “helix measure” . A lot of work

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:31.470
went into that rendering!

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.160
Let’s save a scene.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.370
I’ll call my scene helix.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:42.530
Now, let’s use the scene we saved before
to go back to the overall structure.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:47.910
Reposition the structure to show one of the
sheets and save a quick scene.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.850
The arrows that appear on sheets in PyMOL
point toward the C-terminus.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.949
Notice the strands of the sheet run in opposite
directions.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:01.550
Now, let’s compare this to the other sheet
in the structure.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:05.220
They’re running in the same direction.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.849
This type of beta sheet is called parallel.

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:12.009
Let’s go back to the first sheet using our
scene.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:18.250
This one is called antiparallel—the “anti”
indicates the sheets are running in opposite

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:19.250
directions.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:23.479
Let’s dig into these sheets to see what
this means structurally.

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:28.710
Click on one amino acid in it, and use that
to locate the strand in the sequence.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:35.590
OK, the sheet we want is in Chain B. Select
all of the yellow amino acids by clicking

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.960
and dragging on them in the sequence.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:45.260
Hide unselected, and then rename this selection
“anti”.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.160
Show sticks, and color with CPK coloring.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.199
When I try to move this, it’s a little wonky,
so let’s use the A button to center the

00:06:55.199 --> 00:06:56.349
selection.

00:06:56.349 --> 00:07:00.690
Notice where the side chains are relative
to the backbone atoms.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:05.340
Then, hide the side chains and the cartoon.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:11.199
Now find, polar contacts, just intra main
chain.

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:13.530
There they are!

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:20.350
Notice again these are between a carbonyl
oxygen and the NH of the backbone nitrogen.

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.780
We don’t see the interacting hydrogen atoms.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.889
Go ahead and save a scene.

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:28.310
Onto the parallel sheet.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:32.460
Return to our main scene and click on a residue
within it.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:41.780
Find it in the sequence, and then select all
yellow residues in Chain A. Hide unselected,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:48.310
save the selection by renaming it, and then
zoom.

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:51.889
Show sticks and apply CPK coloring.

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:57.130
Again, we’ll use polar contacts to show
the hydrogen bonds.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:04.840
Actions, find, polar contacts, just intra
main chain.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.460
Hide the side chains, and hide the cartoon.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:15.419
Now, save a scene called parallel.

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:21.069
Using the stick rendering, the way we can
tell this is a parallel beta sheet is by examining

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:26.069
the amide linkages, also called peptide bonds
in a protein.

00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:32.479
Look for the carbonyl on one strand, and find
the nitrogen directly bonded to it.

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:35.229
It’s on the left in this view.

00:08:35.229 --> 00:08:38.900
Now, look at another chain.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.800
This nitrogen is also on the left.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:47.560
The nitrogens are on the same side of the
carbonyls, so the strands are running in the

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.340
same direction: this is a parallel beta sheet.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:55.870
Return to the antiparallel scene now and repeat
this analysis.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:02.200
On this strand that’s positioned near the
top, the nitrogen is to the right of the carbonyl.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:08.100
On the next strand, here’s a carbonyl, and
the adjacent nitrogen is on the left.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:13.070
The strands run in opposite directions in
this antiparallel beta sheet.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.030
And that’s our lesson on measurement and
secondary structure!

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.970
I hope you learned something!

